ry's nominal dollar valuation of silver at each date\n\n\n2. Convert to physical quantity using the statutory rate\n3. Multiply that physical quantity by the \"real\" (inflation-adjusted) silver price to get a value in \"real\" (inflation-adjusted) terms\n\nI'm uncertain about the precise interpretation of \"real inflation-adjusted silver price.\" The calculation requires clarifying whether we're adjusting for historical inflation or converting to a consistent monetary value. The ambiguity lies in whether we're looking for the market price adjusted for inflation or a standardized real value across different time periods.\n\nThe Treasury's fixed rate of $1.29 per ounce seems to conflict with the actual market price, which varied significantly. Historical records show market prices ranging from around $0.40-$0.50 per ounce in the late 1930s to higher values in subsequent decades. This suggests I need to carefully determine the appropriate price to use in my calculation.\n\nI'll investigate potential sources for historical silver price data to resolve this computational challenge.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_5e6808c521f44fe0a2d40ddf","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://datahub.io/core/silver-price\" 2>&1 | grep -A30 \"1938\" | head -40"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
