djusted silver price instead?\n\nSo:\n\n\n1. Silver stock value / $1.29 per oz = physical silver quantity\n2. Physical quantity * inflation-adjusted real silver price = computed value\n\nI'll break down the calculation for each year. For September 1938, I'll divide the monetary stock by the statutory conversion rate to get the physical quantity. Then I'll multiply by the inflation-adjusted price to determine the real value. I'll repeat this process for 1948 and 1958, using the specific conversion rates for each year.\n\nThe goal is to compare the original statutory value with what the silver would be worth at inflation-adjusted market prices. By calculating these three values, I can then determine the median, which will give me a representative estimate of the silver's real worth across these years.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_66ed5c249ffd4845a1040e76","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://www.inflationdata.com/silver-price-history/\" 2>&1 | grep -A30 \"1938\" | head -40"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
