e physical quantities by dividing the monetary stock values by the statutory conversion rate. For 1938, 1948, and 1958, I'll compute the ounces using $1.29 per ounce. Then I'll need to find the historical market prices for each year to adjust for inflation and determine the real value.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_9ada2bee8f494ebdacb4d49e","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://www.gold.org/sites/default/files/reports/Gold%20Prices%20-%2050%20year%20local%20currency%20units.pdf\" 2>&1 | head -30"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
